        <div id="drive-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1/4, 3/8 or 1/2 Inch? A 3/8-Inch Cordless Ratchet Suits Most People
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first decision is drive size, and for most buyers the answer is 3/8 inch. It is the size most car, mower and household fasteners are built around, it has the widest choice of sockets, and every major brand makes a 3/8-inch cordless ratchet. If you searched "3/8 or 1/4 socket size" before landing here, the short version is that 3/8 inch handles roughly 8 mm to 19 mm hardware comfortably, while 1/4 inch is for the small stuff below that. Our <a href="/what-drive-size-socket-set-do-i-need/">guide to socket drive sizes</a> covers the full breakdown.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1/4-Inch Drive: Small Fasteners and Tight Interiors</h3>

          <p>
            A 1/4-inch cordless ratchet is for small fasteners: interior trim, dash panels, hose clamps, sensors and appliance screws, usually 4 mm to 14 mm. The sockets are short and slim, so they reach into places a 3/8-inch socket cannot. The limit is strength. A 1/4-inch drive square is small, and pushing high torque through it is how you snap an extension. If your work is mostly interiors and electronics, a 1/4-inch tool or a ratchet with a swappable 1/4-inch anvil makes sense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3/8-Inch Drive: The Default for Cars and Equipment</h3>

          <p>
            A 3/8-inch drive covers the middle ground, from valve covers and intake bolts to brake caliper slide pins and mower blades on smaller decks. The sockets are still compact enough for engine bays, and the drive is strong enough that the 35 to 75 ft-lb ratings of cordless ratchets are well within its limits. This is why our top pick, the Milwaukee 2567-20, is 3/8 inch only.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1/2-Inch Drive: Bigger Hardware, Not Bigger Cordless Torque</h3>

          <p>
            Half-inch sockets are for larger fasteners such as suspension bolts and lug nuts. The catch is that a cordless ratchet does not become stronger because its anvil is bigger. The Ryobi PCL280K1 has a 1/2-inch anvil but is still rated at 35 ft-lbs. Only the DeWalt DCF510B, at 75 ft-lbs, adds meaningful torque with its 1/2-inch drive. For real 1/2-inch work, you also want an impact wrench.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interchangeable Anvils: Two Sizes in One Tool</h3>

          <p>
            Several picks swap anvils: the Makita models switch between 3/8 and 1/4 inch, the DeWalt DCF520B adds a 1/4-inch hex anvil, and the Ryobi covers all three sizes. This beats using an adapter, which adds length and wobble at the head. If you own both 1/4-inch and 3/8-inch socket sets, a swappable anvil is worth having.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="voltage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 12V or 18V/20V Cordless Ratchet? 12V Fits Tighter Spaces, 20V Adds Torque
          </h2>

          <p>
            The second decision is battery platform, and with ratchets the smaller battery usually wins. A cordless ratchet is held at the end of your arm, often at an awkward angle and often in a gap no wider than your hand. Every inch of battery and every ounce of weight works against you there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12V Ratchets: Shorter, Lighter and Slimmer</h3>

          <p>
            A 12V ratchet such as the Milwaukee 2567-20 or Makita RW01Z is short, light and slim at the grip. The battery slides into the handle rather than hanging off the end, which keeps the overall length down. Torque is usually rated around 35 ft-lbs, which is the right amount for a tool designed to spin fasteners on and off, not to break them loose. For most engine bay and household work, 12V is the better size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">18V and 20V Ratchets: More Torque, More Bulk</h3>

          <p>
            An 18V or 20V ratchet hangs a full-size battery pack off the back of the tool. That costs reach and adds weight, but it allows higher torque ratings: the DeWalt DCF510B is rated at 75 ft-lbs and the DCF520B at 50 ft-lbs. Not every 18V ratchet uses that extra power, though. The Makita XRW01Z and Ryobi PCL280K1 are both rated at 35 ft-lbs, the same as the 12V tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy for the Batteries You Already Own</h3>

          <p>
            In practice, the battery you already own often decides it. A bare-tool ratchet costs far less than starting a new platform, so a DeWalt 20V owner should look at the DCF520B or DCF510B before buying into M12. Our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt comparison</a> explains how the two biggest platforms differ, and the <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi ONE+ review</a> covers the budget platform. If you are starting from nothing, M12 has the deepest range of compact automotive tools.
          </p>

