        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cordless ratchet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Milwaukee 2567-20 M12 FUEL 3/8-inch high-speed ratchet is the best cordless ratchet. The listing rates it at 450 RPM and 35 ft-lbs with a 0.75-inch head profile, so it is fast and slim enough for tight engine bays. DeWalt 20V owners should look at the DCF520B, and anyone needing 1/2-inch drive and more torque at the DCF510B.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a 1/4 or 3/8 cordless ratchet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Get a 3/8-inch cordless ratchet unless most of your work is small interior and trim fasteners. A 3/8-inch drive covers the bulk of car, mower and household hardware and has the widest socket choice. A 1/4-inch tool suits fasteners under about 14 mm. Ratchets with swappable anvils, like the Makita XRW01Z and RW01Z, cover both sizes in one tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cordless ratchet remove lug nuts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Lug nuts are usually tightened well beyond the 35 to 75 ft-lbs that cordless ratchets are rated for, and using the motor to break them loose wears out the tool. Crack lug nuts loose with a breaker bar or use an impact wrench. A cordless ratchet can spin them off once they are loose, though an impact wrench is faster.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 12V or 20V cordless ratchet better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12V cordless ratchet is better for most people because it is shorter, lighter and slimmer, which matters in tight spaces. A 20V ratchet can carry a higher torque rating, like the DeWalt DCF510B's 75 ft-lbs, but it is bulkier. The battery platform you already own is often the deciding factor, since a bare tool costs far less than a new system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are high-speed cordless ratchets worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High-speed ratchets are worth it if you often remove long bolts or many fasteners at once. Rated speeds of 450 RPM or more cut run-down time roughly in half compared with 250 RPM tools. The trade-off is control as a bolt seats, so use a light trigger finger. For occasional short fasteners, a standard-speed ratchet like the Milwaukee 2457-20 is fine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a cordless ratchet manually?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, most can. The Milwaukee 2567-20 and both Makita ratchets are listed as usable as manual ratchets without engaging the motor. This is useful for cracking a stuck fastener loose or giving a final snug turn. Do not put a cheater bar on a cordless ratchet, though, because the head and yoke are not built to take breaker-bar loads.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need impact sockets for a cordless ratchet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A cordless ratchet turns smoothly rather than hammering, so standard chrome sockets are fine and their thinner walls help in tight spaces. Impact sockets are only needed for impact wrenches and impact drivers. Use six-point sockets on fasteners you crack loose by hand, and keep extensions short so the tool's torque reaches the fastener.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

